<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rheinfels Castle</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1245</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Burg Rheinfels</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rheinfels Castle is a ruined castle above <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sankt-goar">Sankt Goar</a> on the left bank of the Middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, built in 1245 by Count Dieter V of Katzenelnbogen to collect Rhine tolls. It withstood a siege by the Rhenish League in 1255–1256, became the residence of Landgrave Philip II of Hesse-Rheinfels and grew into one of the largest fortresses on the Rhine. French revolutionary troops blew it up in 1797.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rheinfels-castle.jpg" alt="Rheinfels Castle" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Phantom3Pix · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Burg_Rheinfels_Bild_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The ruin stands above the left, west bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sankt-goar">Sankt Goar</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland-palatinate">Rhineland-Palatinate</a>. It is the largest castle overlooking the Rhine and once covered five times its present area. The main entrance is a tall square clock or gate tower of about 1300, from which a path along the former moat leads to the remains of the Darmstadt Building, the living quarters of the landgraves of Hesse-Darmstadt, built in Tudor style with pointed gables.</p><p>Beneath the path lies a great cellar, arched over in two phases in 1587–1589. It is 24 metres long and about 16 metres wide and high, with walls up to 4 metres thick, the largest self-supporting vaulted cellar in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and it can hold 400 people. It once housed a wine barrel of 200,000 litres and, restored in 1997, now serves for concerts and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a>. Beside the Darmstadt Building stood a tower 54 metres tall, to which a narrow round top was added in the 14th century.</p><p>The former chapel, the only finished room of the original castle, houses a museum with a model of the castle before its destruction. Beyond it lay the medieval courtyard, with a bakery, pharmacy, garden, brewery, well and livestock that allowed the castle to withstand a long siege. In peacetime 300 to 600 people lived in the complex; during a siege the number could swell to 4,500. Traces of the original white-painted 13th-century plaster survive on some walls.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After its expansions the castle was the largest fortress in the Middle Rhine Valley between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koblenz">Koblenz</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a>. In 1924 the state gave the ruin to the town of St Goar on condition that it was not sold, and in 1998 the town leased it to a nearby hotel; some of the outer buildings now house a hotel, wellness centre and restaurant.</p><p>Georg Friedrich, Prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, claimed that the lease breached the terms of the gift and that the castle should be returned to the Hohenzollern family. A court dismissed the claim in June 2019, and in January 2020 he reached an out-of-court settlement with the town, under which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sankt-goar">St. Goar</a> works with the Princess Kira of Prussia Foundation, a charity for socially disadvantaged young people.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rheinfels Castle” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The counts became wealthy from Rhine tolls collected at castles such as <b>Rheinfels</b> above Sankt Goar, built in 1245, and Burg Katz.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-katzenelnbogen">County of Katzenelnbogen</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 1567 division of the Landgraviate of Hesse among the sons of Philip the Magnanimous, the third son, Philip II, received the Lower County of Katzenelnbogen on the Rhine, with <b>Rheinfels</b> castle above St. Goar as his residence.</blockquote><a href="/state/landgraviate-of-hesse-rheinfels">Landgraviate of Hesse-Rheinfels</a></li></ul></section>
